{"name":"Safety Orange","hex":"#ff6600","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"safety orange","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hue of orange used to set objects apart from their surroundings, particularly in complementary contrast to the azure color of the sky; commonly used in  the US for traffic cones and other construction zone marking."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/safety_orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Safety orange","blocks":[{"text":"Safety orange is a hue used to set objects apart from their surroundings, particularly in complementary contrast to the azure color of the sky. The high-visibility color is commonly used in hunting contexts and for construction site safety."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/94/Sign_Road_Work_Ahead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safety_orange","wikidata":"Q1091453"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"},{"title":"Safety","blocks":[{"text":"Safety is the state of being protected from harm or other danger. Safety can also refer to the control of recognized hazards in order to achieve an acceptable level of risk."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Snake_warning_sign.jpg/330px-Snake_warning_si